 no B2/B3. B1 is terminal, could be useful for outside hand if I collect terminals/honors, but currently not focused.\n- **B5, B6, B7, B8**: These are consecutive Bams; keeping them allows multiple Chow possibilities. B5-B6 could pair with B4 or B7; B7-B8 with B6 or B9. I have B1, B5, B6, B7, B8 \u2013 a spread from 1 to 8. Potentially could aim for a Bams-heavy hand like Half Flush (honors + Bams). Discarding any might reduce that potential.\n- **J3**: Only one, not a pair; dragon but no immediate use.\n- **J1**: Two copies, a potential pair or pung. I should keep J1 as pair candidate.\n\nGiven the across player's two Bams Chows, they might be collecting Bams, so discarding Bams could be risky. But they already have B3-B6 range, so high Bams (B7-B9) might be safer? Not clear. My own Bams are medium numbers (5-8) which are close to their sequences, so possibly dangerous.\n\nReason: To develop the hand efficiently, I should discard tiles that are least useful for forming melds and have low value. T5 and T6 are isolated in Tongs suit with no other Tongs in hand, and Tongs are not prominent in others' melds. Discarding one of them reduces suit clutter and keeps focus on Bams and honors. Between T5 and T6, T5 is slightly more central, but both similar. I choose T5 because T6 might pair with T6 from draws (unshown T6:4) and T5-T6 can become Chow with T4 or T7. But since I aim to reduce Tongs, discard T5. Alternatively, F1 is a wind but only one; discarding it might be safe and avoids giving away a wind Pung chance. However, F1 is prevailing wind, so someone might be collecting winds, but based on history, many winds have been discarded (F3, F2, F4, F1). I think discarding T5 is reasonable to simplify the hand toward Bams/honors.\n\nFinal choice: Play T5.\n\nAnswer: Play T5"}
