gerous and try to improve later. But my hand is already with three exposed melds, so I am committed to trying to win soon. If I cannot win with 8 points, I should try to build a hand that can reach 8. Perhaps I need to aim for \"All Pungs\" or \"Half Flush\" etc. But with current tiles, that's difficult.\n\nLet's check if I can aim for \"Half Flush\": requires only one suit and honors. My melds: W chow, B chow, J pung. So I have two suits, not half flush. To get half flush, I would need to change my B chow to W or honor, but it's exposed, cannot change. So half flush impossible. \"All Pungs\": requires four pungs. I have one pung (J1). I would need three more pungs. That's very unlikely. \"Full Flush\": all one suit, impossible with B and J melds.\n\nGiven the constraints, perhaps the only way to win is to go for a hand that includes patterns like \"Dragon Pung\" (2), \"Self Drawn\" (1), \"Pure Double Chow\" (1), and maybe \"All Simples\"? But I have W1 terminal, so not all simples. \"No Honors\"? I have J1 pung, so no. \n\nWait, maybe I can get \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" for J1 (2 points? Actually, \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" is 1 point. But Dragon Pung is 2 points, so it's better. So J1 gives 2. \n\nIf I win with chow [W1,W2,W3], that includes terminal W1, so not all simples. But maybe \"Mixed Double Chow\"? No.\n\nPerhaps I can get \"Last Tile Draw\" or other situational patterns, but not guaranteed.\n\nGiven that I may not reach 8 points even if I complete, perhaps I should discard the safest tile to avoid giving others a win, and then try to change my hand to a higher scoring pattern by drawing more tiles. But with three exposures, my hand is already limited. Perhaps I should consider discarding W5 to break the pair and try to form a different combination that might yield more points, but that seems speculative.\n\nConsidering all, discarding B3 gives me the best chance to complete a hand quickly, even if it's low scoring. But I must have 8 points to w