annot win even if you complete the pair. So you must ensure your hand meets the 8-point minimum.  \n\nLet\u2019s evaluate potential points if you win with current waits:  \n- Dragon Pung (J1): 2 points.  \n- One Voided Suit (missing T suit): 2 points.  \n- Single Wait (waiting for pair): 1 point.  \n- Self-drawn if you draw winning tile: 1 point.  \n- Possibly No Honors? You have J1 and J2, so no.  \n- All Chows? You have two chows (W and B melds) and one pung (J1), so not All Chows.  \n- All Simples? You have J1 and J2 (honors), so no.  \n- Mixed Double Chow? Not applicable.  \n\nTotal: 2+2+1=5, plus maybe 1 for self-drawn = 6, still short.  \n\nYou might get points for Seat Wind or Prevailing Wind? Your seat wind is North (F4), prevailing wind is East (F1). You have no wind melds.  \n\nSo you cannot win with current hand because it\u2019s below 8 points. Therefore, you must discard in a way that allows you to develop a higher-scoring hand, even if it means breaking tenpati.  \n\nLooking at your hand: You have extra W3, W5, B7. You could try to form more chows or pungs to increase points. For example, if you discard J2, you keep B3 as pair candidate, and you have W3 and W5 which could form a chow with W4? W4 is available (unshown: 1 left), but you already have a meld using W4, so less likely. Alternatively, aim for All Even Pungs? Your tiles are odd numbers mostly.  \n\nGiven the complexity and limited time, perhaps the safest is to discard a tile that doesn\u2019t reduce future flexibility, like B7, since B7 is a middle tile and you already have a B7 in a meld. Discarding B7 keeps both B3 and J2 for pair possibilities, and keeps W3 and W5 for possible chow.  \n\nBut you need to eventually get to 8 points. You might need to go for patterns like Half Flush (only one suit and honors) or All Pungs, etc.  \n\nGiven the situation, I think you should discard a duplicate that is least useful for scoring. B7 is a simple tile in a suit you already have a meld in, so it\u2019s less likely to form new patterns.  \n\nI\u2019ll choose Play B7.  \n\nReason:  \nDiscarding B7 maintains two pair candidates (B3 and J2) and keeps W3 and W5 for potential chow combinations. It doesn\u2019t commit to a specific wait yet and preserves flexibility to develop a higher-scoring hand. Since the current hand is below 8 points, winning now is impossible, so we need to improve the hand\u2019s point potential. B7 is a safe discard considering it\u2019s already used in a meld and is a middle tile with moderate risk.  \n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
